From 47f717e13c96106e0634b9e2b03aa956eb7ea74b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fleet-contributor <fleet-contributor@exe.xyz>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 16:13:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(lanes): make the bead visible before the lane's first
 command claims it (bead-visibility)

A new lane's first act is to claim the bead it was handed, and that claim can fail with 'no issue found
matching <id>' when the bead has not reached this VM's ledger yet - text that is indistinguishable from the
bead not existing. The lane's natural next step is to create a duplicate or report the bead missing, and both
look like new facts afterwards.

Measured (bead-visibility, on a project VM 2026-10-12): the text comes from the CLAIM path (`bd update <id>
--claim`, `bd comments <id>`, `bd close <id>`) rather than from `show`; a fresh LINKED worktree resolves
through the shared git common dir to the canonical ledger (bd info: 494 issues, nothing created locally), so
visibility is a property of BEAD ARRIVAL, not of the worktree. That makes the spawn path the layer where the
condition happens, and the bead id is already known there.

THIS CHANGE: at spawn, if the bead is not visible, pull once and retry; if it is STILL not visible, say so
loudly rather than letting the lane discover it as 'missing'. The normal-case cost is one `bd show`; the pull
happens only when the bead is not visible. The probe runs in the project checkout (`repo_dir`), so it reads
the ledger the lane will read, not the journal's.

RELATED, AND DELIBERATELY NOT DUPLICATED: the fleet bd shim patch (patches/fleet-bd-annotate-missing-bead)
makes the FAILURE say which world it is in, for every caller and every path, including the ones this spawn
hook cannot see. This change removes the cause for the spawned-lane case; the shim patch reports the
condition wherever it still occurs. per-worktree-decoy owns the third part - the guard against a per-worktree
database becoming authoritative.
---
 remote/lanes.sh | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/remote/lanes.sh b/remote/lanes.sh
index be80962..9888306 100755
--- a/remote/lanes.sh
+++ b/remote/lanes.sh
@@ -238,6 +238,24 @@ case $c in
     for l in "${lanes[@]}"; do start_lane "$l"; done ;;
   spawn)
     name=${1:?lane name}; model=${2:-}; bead=${3:-}; role=${4:-implementer}   # empty model = role rotation (pick-model.sh)
+
+    # bead-visibility: MAKE THE BEAD VISIBLE BEFORE THE LANE'S FIRST COMMAND NEEDS IT. A new lane's first act is
+    # to claim the bead it was handed - that is the fleet's rule - and a bead that has not reached this VM's
+    # ledger yet fails with "no issue found matching <id>", which is INDISTINGUISHABLE from the bead not
+    # existing. The lane then creates a duplicate or reports the bead missing, and both look like new facts
+    # afterwards. The id is known HERE, and the fix belongs at the cause rather than in the error text: pull
+    # once, and if it is STILL invisible say so, because that is a different problem (a wrong id, or the
+    # wrong ledger) and the lane must not be told the bead is missing. Measured (bead-visibility): the failing
+    # text comes from the claim path, and a fresh linked worktree resolves to the canonical ledger, so
+    # visibility is a property of bead arrival rather than of the worktree. Cost in the normal case is one
+    # `bd show`; the pull happens only when the bead is not visible.
+    if [ -n "$bead" ]; then
+      if ! ( cd "$(repo_dir)" && bd show "$bead" >/dev/null 2>&1 ); then
+        ( cd "$(repo_dir)" && bd dolt pull >/dev/null 2>&1 ) || true
+        ( cd "$(repo_dir)" && bd show "$bead" >/dev/null 2>&1 ) || \
+          echo "lanes: WARNING (bead-visibility): bead $bead is still not visible to this VM's ledger after a pull - do NOT tell the lane it is missing; check the id, and see 'bd where'" >&2
+      fi
+    fi
     [[ $name =~ ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,30}$ ]] || { echo "bad lane name" >&2; exit 1; }
     [[ $name == coord || $name == merger ]] && { echo "reserved lane name" >&2; exit 1; }
     win_exists "$name" && { echo "$name already running"; exit 0; }
-- 
